  "summary": "The Senate will vote on Supreme Court nominee Neil Gorsuch on April 7, Majority Leader Mitch McConnell announced Tuesday. The vote follows the Senate Judiciary Committee's consideration of the nomination on Monday. Democrats have threatened to filibuster; Republicans hold 52 seats and need 60 votes to end debate, meaning they require at least eight Democrats to join them. As of Monday, Democrats had reached the 41 votes required to sustain a filibuster, according to one tally, while The Hill's Whip List put the number of Democrats opposed at 33. A handful of Democrats plan to vote for cloture to advance the nomination, citing Republican signals that they will invoke the \"nuclear option\" — a rules change allowing confirmation by a simple 51-vote majority — if the filibuster proceeds. Senator Joe Manchin (D-W.Va.) became the first Democrat to announce support for Gorsuch's confirmation; he had previously said he would vote to limit debate. Manchin faces reelection in 2018 in a state President Trump won by double digits. Republicans could confirm Gorsuch without Democratic votes if they change the rules, a prospect that concerns senators in both parties who favor preserving institutional norms.",
