A federal grand jury indicted President Trump's former campaign chairman Paul Manafort and his business and campaign associate Rick Gates on 12 counts unsealed Monday. The charges include conspiracy against the United States, conspiracy to launder money, making false and misleading Foreign Agents Registration Act statements, serving as an unregistered foreign principal, and seven counts of failure to file reports of foreign bank and financial accounts. The 31-page indictment focuses on their work as political consultants and lobbyists for a pro-Russia party in Ukraine, alleging they received tens of millions of dollars and laundered the money through scores of United States and foreign corporations, partnerships, and bank accounts. Special counsel Robert Mueller has been investigating Manafort's financial ties to Ukraine; his Virginia home was searched by the FBI this summer. Manafort and Gates surrendered to federal authorities Monday morning after being told to do so by lawyers working with Mueller, and were scheduled to appear in court at 1:30 p.m. the same day. Following the announcement, Merriam-Webster reported online searches for "indictment" rose 3,600%, "conspiracy" 1,500%, and "collusion" 800% above normal levels.

