{
  "summary": "Hours before the Wednesday Democratic debate in Las Vegas, four of the six candidates slated to participate — former Vice President Joe Biden, former South Bend Mayor Pete Buttigieg, Senator Amy Klobuchar of Minnesota, and Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts — joined picketing Culinary Union members outside the Palms Casino Resort. Billionaire candidate Tom Steyer also visited the picket line. Senators Bernie Sanders of Vermont and former New York City Mayor Michael Bloomberg did not attend. Workers at Stations Casinos and the Palms have voted to unionize but have not secured contracts and are working without them. Stations Casinos is owned by Frank and Lorenzo Fertitta, described as strong supporters of President Donald Trump. Participants chanted \"No justice, no peace!\" and carried signs reading \"No Contracts, No Peace!\"

At the debate Wednesday, Warren attacked Bloomberg, according to live updates from the Nevada caucuses. The caucuses are scheduled for Saturday with a call to caucus at 3 p.m. ET. Bloomberg is not on the Nevada ballot. Buttigieg enters with the most delegates, Sanders won the New Hampshire primary, while Klobuchar and Biden have struggled in earlier contests.

Online sports betting site Bovada listed Sanders as the odds-on favorite for the Democratic nomination at +125, with Bloomberg at +200, a significant improvement from +1000 at the start of the year. Biden and Warren's odds have declined.",
  "conflicts": []
}

Democrat Terry McAuliffe, 56, a former Democratic National Committee chairman and longtime confidant of Bill and Hillary Clinton who had never held elective office, was elected governor of Virginia on Tuesday, defeating Republican Ken Cuccinelli, the state's attorney general. McAuliffe benefited from a larger campaign war chest and framed the race as a referendum on whether Cuccinelli's social views were out of step in an increasingly suburban and diverse state; Cuccinelli appealed almost entirely to the conservative base and was portrayed by McAuliffe as a hardcore tea party favorite. Turnout was low, and both candidates worked through Election Day to reach voters.

The federal health care law dominated the campaign. McAuliffe supported expanding Medicaid to cover 400,000 lower-income Virginians and said the law did not go far enough, favoring a single-payer system. Cuccinelli vehemently opposed the law, was the first to challenge it in court, and on Monday claimed McAuliffe's lead was diminishing because of the law's problems. "I'm scared to death about what Obamacare is doing to Virginians. Terry McAuliffe is scared to death what Obamacare is doing to Terry McAuliffe," Cuccinelli said at a campaign stop. "Tomorrow, we need to have his fears fulfilled." On a Sunday radio program, Cuccinelli also said a McAuliffe administration would threaten Virginians' liberty by forcing doctors to accept Medicaid and Medicare patients.

