Former acting Democratic National Committee Chair Donna Brazile, in excerpts from her forthcoming book published by Politico on Thursday, detailed a joint fundraising agreement signed in August 2015 between the DNC, the Hillary Victory Fund, and Hillary for America. At the time, the party was under Chairwoman Debbie Wasserman Schultz and the primary contest between Hillary Clinton and Sen. Bernie Sanders was underway. Brazile wrote that the agreement turned over control of party financing, strategy, and money to the Clinton campaign, along with hiring decisions: the campaign had the right of refusal for the party communications director and final say on other staff, while the DNC was required to consult the campaign on all staffing, budgeting, data, analytics, and mailings. Brazile said the arrangement "was not illegal, but it sure looked unethical" and wrote that she believes no laws were violated. Party resources are traditionally transferred to the nominee after the primaries conclude. Sanders signed his own joint fundraising agreement with the DNC months later. President Donald Trump responded in a two-part tweet Thursday, alleging without evidence that Clinton and the DNC violated campaign finance and money laundering laws, asking "where is our Justice Department?" and calling the arrangement "real collusion and dishonesty."

