During Tuesday's Democratic presidential debate, moderator Anderson Cooper questioned former Rhode Island governor and senator Lincoln Chafee about his 1999 vote for the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, which repealed the Glass-Steagall Act's separation of commercial and investment banking. Cooper asked how Chafee could press Hillary Clinton on Wall Street ties given that vote. Chafee replied that it was his first vote in the Senate; he had just been appointed to the seat held by his father, John Chafee, after his father's death in 1999. When Cooper asked whether he understood the bill before voting, Chafee said, "I think you're being a little rough."

Later in the debate, Chafee said, "I'm very proud that over my almost 30 years of public service, I have had no scandals. I've always been honest. I have the courage to take the long-term view, and I've shown good judgment. I have high ethical standards." Cooper then noted Chafee had criticized Clinton for using a private email server. Chafee said credibility is an issue for a world leader and "we need someone that has the best in ethical standards as our next president." Asked to respond, Clinton said, "No," drawing loud applause. Vermont senator Bernie Sanders added, "The American people are tired of hearing about your damn emails," and the two shook hands.

