{
  "summary": "President-elect Donald Trump appeared in an interview on \"Fox News Sunday\" with Chris Wallace that aired Sunday. In the interview, Trump said \"nobody really knows\" the facts about climate change and called it \"not something that's hard and fast,\" despite widespread scientific consensus. He said he was \"studying\" the Paris agreement, a joint effort by nearly 200 countries to reduce greenhouse gas emissions, and added, \"I don't want that agreement to put us at a competitive disadvantage with other countries.\" Trump had met earlier in the week with Leonardo DiCaprio to discuss global warming and named Oklahoma Attorney General Scott Pruitt, a defender of fossil fuel interests, to head the Environmental Protection Agency. In 2012, Trump had tweeted that global warming \"was created by and for the Chinese in order to make U.S. manufacturing non-competitive.\" 

During the same interview, Trump rejected a CIA report that Russia's hacks were aimed at helping him win the election. \"I think it's ridiculous. I think it's just another excuse. I don't believe it,\" he said. The comments came after Trump lashed out at NBC News on Twitter following a segment on the CIA assessment.

On Friday, Wallace previewed the interview on Fox News Channel's \"Shepard Smith Reports,\" saying Trump was acting as if he were already commander-in-chief. Wallace, who has covered presidential transitions since Ronald Reagan in 1980, said \"this is just as different\" and that Trump \"is kind of the commander-in-chief already,\" noting that Trump's statements on China, jobs, and economic policy were receiving more traction than those of President Obama before the January 20 inauguration.",
  "conflicts": []
}

Secret Service Director Julia Pierson testified Tuesday before the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform about a September 19 security breach in which Omar J. Gonzalez, an Army veteran armed with a knife, scaled the White House fence and entered the executive mansion. Before the hearing, Pierson wrote to committee chairman Darrell Issa, Republican of California, requesting that most of the discussion take place in a closed briefing. She wrote that a comprehensive discussion of Secret Service policies would require disclosures that are "at the very least law enforcement sensitive and, in some instances, classified," and that publicly airing security measures would "arm those who desire to cause injury — or worse — to the president and first family," which she called "beyond reckless." Committee members said Monday they did not believe Pierson should move the discussion behind closed doors to avoid embarrassing details. Representative Jason Chaffetz, Republican of Utah and a committee member, said, "She's got a lot to answer for."

During the hearing, Pierson said the breach was unacceptable and that "mistakes were made" by agents. She said the security plan at the White House was "not properly executed" when Gonzalez jumped the fence and entered the building. Lawmakers said Gonzalez made it much farther into the White House than officials previously disclosed. Pierson said a review would help the agency identify the facts, learn from them, and make changes to the agency's response, and she vowed that such a breach would "never happen again."

