Republican National Convention rules committee members approved two tentative rule changes Friday in Tampa. One would penalize states holding presidential primaries earlier than the party schedule allows. Florida, which moved its primary to January 31, had its delegation reduced from 99 to 50 voting delegates under existing rules. The proposed change would limit future violators to no more than 12 voting delegates. The second change would bind convention delegate allocation to the results of each state's Republican primary or caucus. Mitt Romney allies said primary voters expect delegates to support the primary winner. Ron Paul won no primaries but had accumulated approximately 175 delegates through state party meetings and conventions.

Paul will not speak at the convention nor be formally nominated. His campaign fell short of securing the five-state plurality of unbound delegates required for a floor nomination. In Maine, the convention credentials committee awarded half of Paul's 20 delegates to Romney supporters. Paul spokesman Jesse Benton said, "Dr. Paul won't be nominated because [we] came up short on winning five states of unbound delegates, not because of any lawyers." He added, "We negotiated, fought and got additional delegates seated for Dr. Paul." Paul's team continued to contest the Maine delegation seating, and Nevada delegates said they still planned to nominate Paul. The formal nomination vote for Romney was moved to Monday amid concern about a possible disruption. Paul delegates expressed satisfaction with the party platform and their inclusion in the process. A tribute video for Paul was scheduled for Tuesday night. Both rule changes remained subject to final votes by the committee and the full convention.

