President Barack Obama delivered his farewell address on Tuesday night in Chicago. During the speech he teared up while paying tribute to his wife, Michelle Obama. "Michelle," Obama said, wiping away tears, "for the past twenty-five years, you have not only been my wife and mother of my children; you have been my best friend." The first lady and their elder daughter, Malia, sitting in the front row, also shed tears. Obama continued: "You took on a role you didn't ask for and you made it your own with grace and with grit and with style and good humor. You made the White House a place that belongs to everybody." He praised his daughters for becoming "amazing" young women even as they "wore the burden of years in the spotlight so easily." "Of all that I've done in my life, I'm most proud to be your dad," he said. Obama also addressed Vice President Joe Biden: "You were the first choice I made as a nominee, and the best. Not just because you have been a great vice president, but because in the bargain, I gained a brother. We love you and Jill like family, and your friendship has been one of the great joys of our life." After the speech, Obama hugged Malia on stage, then embraced Biden.

A letter to the editor published Jan. 11 wrote that Obama's "dignity, wit, graciousness and intelligence will be deeply missed" and that he "accomplished much these past eight years in spite of Republicans out to stop him from Day 1." The writer called the Nov. 4, 2008, election night in Chicago a "cherished moment." On CNN's "New Day," former White House Press Secretary Jay Carney said Obama's presidency was "scandal-free beyond his personal life" and that "the man's integrity, his decency and his optimism about America that he both embodied and pursued, I think will be his legacy."

