Virginia Gov. Terry McAuliffe spoke at a McClatchy event on the third day of the Democratic National Convention in Philadelphia. He said Hillary Clinton opposes the Trans-Pacific Partnership: "You've got TPP, which Hillary is against it... She and I disagree on that... we're friends, but we don't always agree." He added, "She's against it, and the president is for it... Hillary's been very clear: She cannot support the TPP in its present form."

Two days earlier, McAuliffe told Politico he thought Clinton would change her position after the election, saying she supported the deal but wanted fixes. He said: "I worry that if we don't do TPP, at some point China's going to break the rules — but Hillary understands this... Once the election's over, and we sit down on trade, people understand [there are] a couple [of] things we want to fix on it, but going forward we've got to build a global economy."

On Wednesday, McAuliffe said: "She will not change on TPP, but the point is, [could] we get a trade deal that Hillary would negotiate where it's higher wages, income up, and worker protections and we create more jobs and protect national security — Hillary'd be for that. That's my point... She can't get there, probably, on TPP, but that doesn't mean she's against all trade deals... But she doesn't like TPP. She can't get comfortable with that."

The Clinton campaign said Tuesday she would oppose the agreement both before and after the election. Donald Trump said: "The day she gets in, which hopefully will never happen, she will approve TPP and that will be another disaster," arguing Clinton switched her stance only because of his opposition. Clinton had faced pressure from Democratic grassroots and Sanders supporters, who made opposition to trade deals a campaign cornerstone and protested at the convention with anti-TPP signs. McAuliffe later said he only hoped Clinton would support the deal.

