President Obama formally nominated Sharon Block and Richard Griffin Jr. to the National Labor Relations Board after a U.S. Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit ruled their January 2012 recess appointments unconstitutional. The three-judge panel decided on January 25, 2013, that the Senate was not legally in recess when Obama appointed them. Block, former labor counsel to Senator Edward M. Kennedy, and Griffin, former general counsel for the International Union of Operating Engineers, have served on the board since January 2012. Forty Senate Republicans sent a letter demanding they immediately leave the board, withdraw from all activities, and stop drawing salaries and benefits. Senate Republicans said the ruling casts doubt on recent NLRB decisions and indicated they will await resolution of the case before acting on the nominations. Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell said, "Our lawyer believes it's highly likely the Supreme Court will take the case and it will be interesting how this comes out." Obama also transmitted the nomination of Richard Cordray, former Ohio attorney general, to head the Bureau of Consumer Financial Protection; Republicans have previously blocked Cordray over disputes about the bureau's powers.

