Bill Maher, Michael Avenatti and Chuck Todd each reacted on Friday to a court ruling that forced President Trump’s personal attorney Michael Cohen to identify Fox News host Sean Hannity as a third, previously undisclosed client. On HBO’s “Real Time,” Maher called the Trump–Hannity relationship a “violation” of the Fourth Estate, adding, “It’s not supposed to be a branch of the First Estate.” Maher said he understood that Hannity “obviously carries Trump’s water every day on the air,” but was “slightly more stunned at the revelation that Hannity reportedly talks with the president every day. Can you imagine if they found out that (former President Barack) Obama was talking to Rachel Maddow every day?” Avenatti, representing Stormy Daniels, appeared on the same program and predicted that documents bearing Hannity’s name would prove “very embarrassing to Sean Hannity,” adding that Cohen “knows where almost all the bodies are buried” and would “sing like a canary” because “this guy is neither tough nor smart … a zero.” Daniels alleges a pre-presidency affair with Trump and a $130,000 payment arranged by Cohen; she is suing to void the nondisclosure agreement. Hannity has denied keeping Cohen on retainer, saying he consulted him only on real estate matters. On WABC’s “Bernie and Sid in the Morning,” Todd criticized Fox for not requiring Hannity to disclose the connection when discussing the FBI raids on Cohen’s office: “I’m surprised Fox isn’t making him, at a minimum, disclose a conflict every time he talks about this.” Todd also objected to a post on Hannity’s website titled “The Utter Hypocrisy of Chuck Todd” that highlighted his wife’s work as a Democratic consultant, saying, “That’s my wife, that’s not me. I think it’s a ridiculous place that we’re in society that it’s guilt by association.”

