Special Counsel Robert Mueller's team sent President Trump's lawyers a list of potential interview topics, CNN reported. The topics include Trump's reported request to former FBI Director James Comey to drop the investigation into former National Security Adviser Michael Flynn, which Trump called a "Comey lie," and Trump's outreach to lawmakers about congressional investigations into Russian meddling in the 2016 election. Mueller is conducting a criminal investigation into possible collusion between the Trump campaign and Russia. The investigation has thus far led to two indictments and two guilty pleas. CNN also reported that Mueller intends to speak to former White House chief strategist Stephen Bannon about Comey and Flynn.

Speaking with reporters on Wednesday, Trump said he would "love to" speak with Mueller under oath and is "looking forward to it, actually." He stated there was "no collusion whatsoever" and "no obstruction whatsoever." Trump said he would like to talk to Mueller as soon as possible and expects the interview to occur in the next two to three weeks, but added that no date is set and his decision is subject to his lawyers. Mueller declined to comment to The Hill.

According to four people told of the matter, Trump ordered the firing of Mueller last June but backed down after White House counsel Donald McGahn threatened to resign rather than carry out the directive. The West Wing confrontation marks the first time Trump is known to have tried to fire the special counsel. Mueller learned about the episode in recent months as his investigators interviewed current and former senior White House officials in his inquiry into whether the president obstructed justice. Amid the first wave of media reports that Mueller was examining a possible obstruction case, Trump began to argue that Mueller had three conflicts of interest: a dispute years ago over fees at Trump National Golf Club in Sterling, Virginia, that prompted Mueller, then FBI director, to resign his membership; Mueller's recent work for the law firm that previously represented Trump's son-in-law Jared Kushner; and Mueller's interview to return as FBI director the day before he was appointed special counsel in May.

